{# One server-rendered charge row inside a sales document's totals box — the same markup order's
   #order-charge-row-template and the quote's #estimate-charge-row-template clone client-side
   (.order-charge-line-row, so both pick up order's styling verbatim), rendered by the server so a
   saved document's charges are visible and editable without JavaScript.

   Shared by templates/admin/order/form.html.twig and templates/admin/estimate/form.html.twig
   because these two forms diverging is exactly how a no-JS admin ended up able to edit a quote's
   charges and not an order's: the quote form grew this row server-side, order never did, and
   nothing made the two disagree visible. One definition cannot drift.

   `hook` is 'order' or 'estimate' — the only thing that genuinely differs between the two forms,
   since app.js keys its per-form handlers off .js-order-* and .js-estimate-* class names.

   `type` is 'shipping', 'fee' or 'tax' — the same word the line each row becomes uses — and the
   row posts itself back as charge_lines[index][label|amount|type|slug|taxClass|placement], which
   SalesDocumentChargeLines turns into that line.

   Charges picked from the bottom Add Line select carry a fixed label; ones added through "Empty
   Shipping/Tax/Fee Line" are free text, so those keep an editable label input. #}
{% macro charge_row(hook, index, label, amount, type, slug, taxClass, placement) %}
    {% set isNamedShipping = type == 'shipping' and (label == 'Custom Shipping' or label matches '/^Shipping \\(.+\\)$/') %}
    {% set isBuiltIn = isNamedShipping or label starts with 'Custom ' %}
    {% set placementLabels = {'main_line': 'Main Line', 'before_tax_line': 'Before Tax', 'after_tax_line': 'After Tax'} %}
    <div class="order-charge-line-row" data-charge-type="{{ type }}"{% if isNamedShipping %} data-charge-kind="named"{% endif %}>
        <span>
            <button type="button" class="mini-action danger js-only js-{{ hook }}-remove-charge" aria-label="Remove charge">&#10005;</button>
            {# The no-JS twin. The ✕ above is a type="button" and therefore a dead control without
               JavaScript, which left an admin able to zero a charge's amount but never delete the
               row. This one is a real submit: it drops this row, saves and recalculates in one
               POST, the same shape as the Add Line submit on the bar below.

               Carries its own index as the value, because that is the only thing that identifies
               which row was pressed — the rows are a posted array and every one of them posts. #}
            <button type="submit" class="mini-action danger no-js-inline" name="remove_charge_line" value="{{ index }}" aria-label="Remove charge and save">&#10005;</button>
            {% if isBuiltIn %}
                <span class="order-charge-label-text">{{ label }}</span>
                <input type="hidden" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][label]" value="{{ label }}">
            {% else %}
                <input type="text" class="order-charge-label-input" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][label]" value="{{ label }}" placeholder="Enter label...">
            {% endif %}
            {# Tax and fee rows become lines, and a line wants a stable reporting key that survives
               a relabel. Optional — blank derives it from the label. #}
            {% if type != 'shipping' %}
                <input type="text" class="order-charge-slug-input" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][slug]" value="{{ slug|default('') }}" placeholder="slug (optional)">
            {% endif %}
            {# A fee line has a tax class and a placement. A shipping row takes the document's
               highest class and main_line at save; a row typed here has no Fee definition to
               inherit either from, so the admin picks them.

               On the order form (hook == 'order') placement is a hidden field, not a select
               (#669, order-only): it used to be editable right here, any time after the row
               existed, and editing it never actually moved the row — the totals box has no "Main
               Line" region to move a Before Tax row into, so changing the value just changed what
               would happen on the NEXT save, with nothing on screen saying so. The order's add-line
               bar now asks for placement once, before a fresh row exists; this field only
               round-trips that choice (or, for a row the server is redrawing after a save, what is
               already stored). The quote keeps its own select and its own JS below — untouched.

               Beside the hidden field, a plain-text label states the same choice: the row's own
               DOM position no longer says which it is — Main Line and Before Tax both anchor
               beside Subtotal (chargeRowAnchor() in app.js), because both feed the same pre-tax
               total today — so a row that carries no visible statement of its own placement is
               indistinguishable from the other. #}
            {% if type == 'fee' %}
                <select class="order-charge-select-input" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][taxClass]" aria-label="Fee tax class">
                    {% for code in ['E', 'G', 'S'] %}
                        <option value="{{ code }}"{{ (taxClass|default('E')) == code ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ code }}</option>
                    {% endfor %}
                </select>
                {% if hook == 'order' %}
                    <span class="order-charge-placement-label">{{ placementLabels[placement|default('main_line')] }}</span>
                    <input type="hidden" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][placement]" value="{{ placement|default('main_line') }}">
                {% else %}
                    <select class="order-charge-select-input" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][placement]" aria-label="Fee placement">
                        {% for option in [{value: 'main_line', text: 'Main Line'}, {value: 'before_tax_line', text: 'Before Tax'}, {value: 'after_tax_line', text: 'After Tax'}] %}
                            <option value="{{ option.value }}"{{ (placement|default('main_line')) == option.value ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ option.text }}</option>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </select>
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}
        </span>
        {# min="0" stays, and now means the same thing on both forms.
           SalesDocumentChargeLines::normalize() floors a negative amount at 0.0, so the server
           enforces exactly what this attribute states and the constraint does not lie.

           Deliberately the opposite call from the line quantity, which lost its min in the same
           sweep (#248). The difference is what happens after the coercion: a coerced QUANTITY is
           reported — SalesDocumentLineWarnings names the line and paints the cell red — and that
           explanation is only reachable if the figure is allowed to reach the server. A coerced
           charge amount has no such mechanism; it just becomes 0.00 with nothing said. Between a
           silent rewrite and the browser saying so before the post, the browser is the better of
           two behaviours that agree on the outcome.

           This markup is shared by the order and quote forms, so the two cannot drift back apart —
           which is the whole reason the row was extracted here (#241). Until #248 the attribute was
           present in both and enforced in neither-then-one: the order form's novalidate switched it
           off there and nowhere else. #}
        <label>$ <input type="number" class="js-{{ hook }}-charge-amount" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][amount]" min="0" step="0.01" value="{{ amount|number_format(2, '.', '') }}"></label>
        <input type="hidden" name="charge_lines[{{ index }}][type]" value="{{ type }}">
    </div>
{% endmacro %}
